# Target C: seven-no-root `D=-1,k=3,d=0,p=1` closure

Date: 2026-09-20

Status: exact finite closure of the seven-no-root

`D=-1, k=3, d=0, p=1`

rank-density branch. This note also corrects the scope of the earlier direct six-shadow audit: its touch-count filters cover only the subcase in which the unique singleton remains touched after every pair deletion. The closure below includes singleton touch sizes one and two as well.

Companion verifier:

`research/verify_target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_seven_noroot_k3_dminus1_corrected_2026_09_20.py`

## 1. Input from the deletion theorems

Let `B` be a hypothetical seven-no-root counterexample in this branch. The established one-row and depth-two synchronization results give a rank-four coefficient-constraint space

`P_7 <= Q^7`

such that deleting any one or any two of the seven external coordinates preserves rank four.

Equivalently,

`min support(P_7) >= 3`.

Every two-row deletion therefore has

`D=3, k=1`.

The total root-row parameters remain `d=0,p=1` under deletion because the root-row set itself is fixed. The unique incomplete root row may, however, become *untouched*. This distinction matters: total `p=1` does not imply touched incomplete cost `p_t=1` in every deletion shadow.

The earlier direct verifier imposed touch count at least three and therefore did not by itself certify the low-touch subcases.

## 2. Why one touched five-row shadow always exists

First, the singleton root row cannot be untouched by all seven no-root cycles. If it were globally untouched, it would impose no external coefficient constraint and contribute no relative `T` relation. Removing that inert root row would lower `|A|` by one without changing `delta`, producing a seven-no-root state with

`D=-2, k=3, d=0, p=0`,

which is already closed by the committed `D=-2,k=3` theorem.

Thus the singleton is touched by at least one of the seven no-root cycles.

Now choose two no-root rows to delete while keeping the singleton touched.

- If the singleton touch support has size at most five, at least two no-root rows do not touch it; delete two such rows.
- If the touch support has size six or seven, deleting any two rows leaves at least four or five touching rows respectively.

Hence every hypothetical seven-row state has at least one five-row pair-deletion shadow in which the singleton remains touched.

That shadow has exactly

`D=3, k=1, d=0, p=1`

with a touched singleton. It is enough to enumerate these exact five-row boundary shadows and add the two deleted no-root rows back in all possible ways. No lower bound on the singleton touch count is needed during either extension.

## 3. Exact five-row boundary

For each of the 20,451 `S_7` orbit representatives of five no-root cycles, the verifier uses the committed exact-rational coefficient-space enumeration to construct every canonical rank-four `p=1,d=0` state with a specified touched singleton.

For such a five-row state the coefficient nullspace has dimension one. The verifier constructs the unique external relation lift in the 15-dimensional `T` space, adjoins the complete-block relation rows, and computes the exact defect.

The full canonical `d=0` census is

```text
canonical d=0 states                 639,484
D=3 boundary states                    1,030
D=6 states                            638,454
```

The maximum squared norm of any relation row in the exact rerank is 84.

All 1,030 `D=3` states are retained as extension parents.

## 4. Corrected six-row extension

Each of the 1,030 exact five-row parents is extended by every possible sixth no-root cycle. The extension search requires:

- rank-four coefficient space;
- projection back to the exact parent coefficient space;
- rank four after every single-coordinate deletion;
- canonical coefficient generation with the same singleton block and singleton state;
- complete-block deficiency zero.

There is deliberately no singleton-touch filter.

The exact computation gives

```text
five-row D=3 parents                    1,030
raw six-row extensions                103,000
raw coefficient candidates                817
normalized six-row states                 705
distinct six-cycle sets                   267
```

The raw singleton-touch census is

```text
touch 1      106
touch 2       78
touch 3      430
touch 4      191
touch 5        9
touch 6        3
```

The touch-one and touch-two states are precisely the cases omitted by the old direct audit. They are retained here.

The maximum intermediate coefficient-space count was 43 and the maximum number of eligible local blocks was 13.

## 5. Corrected seven-row extension

The 705 normalized six-row states are then extended by every possible seventh no-root cycle, again with no touch-count rejection.

The exact computation gives

```text
six-row parents                              705
raw extensions                            69,795
single-deletion synchronized candidates      908
duplicate paths                               86
unique seven-row candidates                  822
```

The singleton-touch census among the 822 unique candidates is

```text
touch 1        7
touch 3      388
touch 4      405
touch 6       16
touch 7        6
```

Thus low-touch seven-row candidates really do occur. This confirms that the old `touch >= 3` rejection was not logically harmless as a coverage argument and needed to be removed.

For each of the 822 candidates the verifier computes the coefficient-space rank after all 21 pair punctures. Every candidate has minimum pair-projection rank exactly three rather than the required four:

```text
minimum pair-projection rank 3      822
minimum pair-projection rank 4        0
```

The number of bad coordinate pairs per candidate has census

```text
1 bad pair       24
2 bad pairs      33
4 bad pairs     406
6 bad pairs     354
7 bad pairs       5
```

Therefore

`depth-two survivors = 0`.

No final seven-row `T`-space rerank is needed: every coefficient candidate already contradicts the proved pair-puncturing synchronization condition.

## 6. Exactness and coverage

The coefficient-space operations in the corrected extension stages are the exact rational row-space operations inherited from the committed seven-no-root `D=-2` verifier.

The five-row `T` rerank uses the established prime `2^127-1`. Complete-block relation rows have squared norm at most 12 and the largest exact external relation row encountered has squared norm 84. The verifier checks Lucas-Lehmer primality and

`(2^127-1)^2 > 84^15`.

Hadamard therefore guarantees that the modular rank decisions agree with rational rank.

Coverage does not assume that every pair-deletion shadow keeps the singleton touched. It uses only the existence of one touched five-row shadow, proved in Section 2. Starting from that shadow, the two deleted rows are restored exhaustively. The six- and seven-row searches allow touch sizes one and two and impose only necessary coefficient-space, deficiency, and projection conditions. Hence every hypothetical branch counterexample maps into the enumerated candidate set.

The final candidate set is empty after the necessary 21 pair-puncture tests. Therefore

> No seven-no-root state with `D=-1,k=3,d=0,p=1` exists.

This closes the entire `D=-1,k=3` arithmetic boundary case.

## 7. Relation to the earlier direct verifier

`research/verify_target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_seven_noroot_k3_dminus1_direct_2026_09_20.py`

remains useful as an independent audit of the `touch >= 3` subcase, but its explicit touch-count filters should not be cited as a proof of the full branch. The corrected verifier in this note is the branch-level closure certificate.

The earlier depth-two theorem itself remains valid. If a pair deletion in the rejected `s=1` alternative made the singleton untouched, stripping that inert row would turn the five-no-root `D=0` shadow into a five-no-root state of defect `-1`, contradicting the already established complete five-no-root density theorem. If the singleton remains touched, the committed `k=2,p=1` slack theorem rules out the same `D=0` alternative.

## 8. Remaining seven-no-root cases

This result does not close the entire seven-no-root layer. The original arithmetic reduction has 30 boundary tuples. The already closed cases now include

- `D=-2,k=3,d=0,p=0`;
- both `D=-2,k=4` cases `(d,p)=(0,3),(1,0)`;
- `D=-1,k=3,d=0,p=1`.

The next work should use the same deletion-depth and generalized-weight machinery on the `k>=4` and `k>=5` arithmetic boundary cases rather than restart a blind 4.55-million-orbit scan.

The direct C-II endpoint remains unchanged at

`3874 = 3076 C7 + 798 (C3 disjoint-union C4)`.

No theorem-state authority file is changed by this branch closure.